<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kokstad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kokstad is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, founded in 1869 at the foot of Mount Currie by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-iii">Adam Kok III</a> and named after him. It was the capital of Griqualand East until the territory was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> in 1879, and it remains a centre of Griqua identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kokstad.jpg" alt="Kokstad" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A3alb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kokstad_City_Hall_and_Victorian_Bandstand._(Cast_Iron)._Also_Memorial_to_Cape_Mounted_Riflemen_%26_Volunteers,_East_Griqualand._1899_-1902._03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town lies on the outer slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> at 1,302 m above sea level, with Mount Currie rising behind it to 2,224 m. Stad is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> word for city. It stands about 10 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> border on the N2 national road, which runs east to Port Shepstone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> and south to Mthatha. The surrounding countryside is mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> farms, and the town is a centre for cheese and other dairy products.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> had split at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griquatown">Griquatown</a> in 1820, and one section under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-iii">Adam Kok III</a> moved first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippolis">Philippolis</a>. Facing growing conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>, who took Griqua land on lease and refused to return it, several hundred Griquas crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> by Ongeluks Nek in 1861 into a region known as Nomansland. They arrived eighteen months later exhausted, most of their livestock dead, and named the mountain after Sir Walter Currie, who had supported their settlement. Each Griqua man could secure a farm of 3,000 acres, but most sold their land cheaply to white settlers.</p><p>In 1869 the Reverend William Dower agreed to found a mission for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> on condition that they resettle on the banks of the Mzimhlava river. In 1874 the trading store of George Brisley and Donald Strachan, Strachan and Co, issued trade tokens that circulated as money across a wide region, described as the first indigenous currency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Adam Kok died after a fall from a cart in 1875. After East Griqualand passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, the town gained its first hotel, The Royal, and in 1881 a newspaper, the Kokstad Advertiser, both started by an African-American. It became a municipality in 1892, and in 1904 a third of its 2903 people were Griquas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town was transferred to Natal province in 1978 and lies in the Harry Gwala District Municipality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, with just over 50,000 people. Its heritage sites include the Adam Kok Monument, a cast-iron bandstand of 1912 declared a national monument in 1983, and the old town hall, while the East Griqualand Museum is devoted chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kokstad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They founded <b>Kokstad</b> in 1869 below Mount Currie.</blockquote><a href="/state/griqualand-east">Griqualand East</a></li></ul></section>
